# 6. Quantitative investment decision framework

Each location should be evaluated using a five-year total-cost and risk-adjusted business case.

## Core financial model

For each product-country-destination combination, calculate:

\[
\text{Landed Cost} =
\text{Manufacturing Cost}
+ \text{Inbound Logistics}
+ \text{Outbound Logistics}
+ \text{Tariffs}
+ \text{Taxes and Fees}
+ \text{Inventory Cost}
+ \text{Quality and Warranty Cost}
+ \text{Compliance Cost}
+ \text{Allocated Fixed Cost}
\]

Then calculate:

\[
\text{Risk-Adjusted NPV} =
\text{Base-Case NPV}
- \text{Expected Disruption Cost}
- \text{Qualification Cost}
- \text{Transition Cost}
- \text{Stranded-Asset Risk}
\]

The model should include:

- Greenfield versus brownfield capex;
- Ramp-up scrap and rework;
- Training and expatriate support;
- Supplier-development spending;
- Dual inventory during transition;
- Working capital;
- Tax incentives after expiry;
- Utility reliability and backup power;
- Freight variability;
- Tariff scenarios;
- FX exposure;
- Labor inflation;
- Customer requalification;
- Warranty risk; and
- Exit or conversion value of the facility.

## Suggested investment gates

### Gate 1: Network screen

Advance only sites that satisfy:

- Minimum tariff/origin benefit;
- Sufficient labor availability;
- Acceptable logistics;
- Feasible utilities;
- No disqualifying legal or political risk; and
- A credible path to customer approval.

### Gate 2: Supplier and process validation

Advance only after:

- Critical supplier mapping;
- Quality audits;
- Sample production;
- Process-capability studies;
- Labor-market validation;
- Origin calculations;
- Site-specific incentive confirmation; and
- A realistic ramp plan.

### Gate 3: Pilot economics

Approve production investment only if the pilot demonstrates:

- Target quality;
- Stable output;
- Acceptable cycle time;
- Documented origin qualification;
- Reliable logistics;
- Trained supervisors;
- Customer acceptance; and
- Positive risk-adjusted economics under downside scenarios.

### Gate 4: Scale decision

Scale only where:

- Demand is sufficient;
- Capacity utilization will exceed the company’s minimum threshold;
- The second site reduces a clearly measured concentration risk;
- Supplier localization has reached the required level; and
- The organization can support the additional complexity.

