# 7. Managing engineering resistance and organizational feasibility

Engineering resistance is a material execution risk, not merely a communications problem. Engineers may reasonably fear:

- Loss of product quality;
- Repeated line-debugging work;
- Uncontrolled engineering changes;
- Weak local technical capability;
- Duplicate tooling;
- Increased warranty exposure;
- Fragmented accountability; and
- Loss of control over product standards.

## A. Use modular product and process architecture

Before moving production, divide products into:

- Common global modules;
- Configurable regional modules;
- Country-specific adaptations;
- Safety-critical modules;
- Customer-specific options; and
- Final-assembly and test operations.

Design transfer packages should include:

- Frozen product configurations;
- Standard work instructions;
- Tooling specifications;
- Quality-control plans;
- Digital work instructions;
- Test protocols;
- Calibration requirements;
- Control plans;
- Approved supplier lists; and
- Escalation rules.

## B. Establish one design authority

The company should avoid separate country engineering teams making inconsistent changes.

Create a **Regional Manufacturing Transfer Office** led jointly by operations and engineering. It should control:

- Product release;
- Process changes;
- Tooling changes;
- Supplier substitutions;
- Deviation approvals;
- Qualification evidence;
- Training standards; and
- Transfer readiness.

Local plants can improve processes within defined boundaries, but design and safety authority remains controlled.

## C. Use a “golden line” and replication model

Keep the original East Asian line as the reference or “golden line” for:

- First production of new products;
- Process validation;
- Benchmark cycle times;
- Quality comparison;
- Training;
- Tooling verification; and
- Engineering problem resolution.

Replicate validated cells rather than designing every ASEAN plant independently.

## D. Make engineers co-owners of the transition

Assign senior engineers to:

- Select products for transfer;
- Define non-negotiable process characteristics;
- Train ASEAN teams;
- Approve first articles;
- Conduct layered process audits;
- Review early field performance; and
- Lead supplier development.

Tie engineering objectives to successful transfer quality and time, not simply to retaining production in the incumbent hub.

## E. Provide organizational safeguards

Use:

- A single global bill-of-materials system;
- Common ERP and manufacturing-execution standards;
- Shared quality metrics;
- Cross-site production reviews;
- Common certification requirements;
- A formal escalation path;
- Short-term rotations;
- Local technical academies; and
- Clear accountability for quality and warranty outcomes.

The message to engineering should be that the program is **controlled replication and resilience**, not uncontrolled fragmentation.

