# 8. Phased implementation roadmap

## Phase 0: Strategic diagnosis — 0 to 3 months

Complete:

- Product-family segmentation;
- Destination-market analysis;
- HS-code validation;
- Tariff and origin mapping;
- Supplier concentration analysis;
- Labor and skills study;
- Logistics and utilities assessment;
- Political and regulatory review;
- Engineering-readiness assessment;
- Incentive prequalification; and
- Organization and governance design.

### Deliverable

A country-by-product-destination matrix showing:

- Current landed cost;
- Preferential tariff eligibility;
- Minimum origin threshold;
- Required localization;
- Qualification complexity;
- Capex;
- Ramp time; and
- Risk-adjusted business case.

## Phase 1: Pilot design — 3 to 9 months

Select:

- One standardized product family for Vietnam;
- One complex product or integration cell for Thailand;
- One specialist electronics or test module for Malaysia, if justified;
- A local-market assembly or service pilot in Indonesia or the Philippines, if demand supports it.

At this stage:

- Lease or acquire limited capacity;
- Use contract manufacturing where appropriate;
- Transfer mature products rather than new platforms;
- Keep high-risk components from the incumbent hub;
- Establish origin documentation;
- Train local supervisors and quality personnel; and
- Conduct customer and regulatory approvals.

## Phase 2: Controlled production — 9 to 18 months

Launch limited commercial production with:

- Dual production at the incumbent and ASEAN sites;
- 100% inspection for critical features initially;
- Parallel customer acceptance testing;
- Buffer inventory;
- Expatriate and engineering support;
- Weekly quality and delivery reviews;
- Supplier corrective-action programs; and
- Formal ramp-down criteria for the old site.

Do not transfer full volume until the new plant achieves sustained performance across multiple production cycles.

## Phase 3: Regional scale — 18 to 36 months

Expand only validated product families.

Potential actions include:

- Move standard assembly to Vietnam;
- Move complex integration and machining to Thailand;
- Add Malaysian controls and testing capability;
- Establish regional spare-parts centers;
- Introduce ASEAN supplier development programs;
- Add local-market assembly in Indonesia where local-content economics justify it; and
- Build cross-site capacity balancing.

## Phase 4: Network optimization — 36 to 60 months

Reassess:

- Tariffs and trade agreements;
- Actual origin qualification;
- Supplier performance;
- Labor inflation;
- Capacity utilization;
- Quality and warranty;
- Customer demand;
- Incentive expiry;
- Political risk; and
- Engineering workload.

At this stage, the company may choose to:

- Expand one ASEAN hub;
- Add a second source for critical components;
- Consolidate underperforming capacity;
- Shift products between Thailand and Vietnam;
- Localize more content; or
- Retain a flexible contract-manufacturing option.

