# 9. Scenario triggers and contingency actions

The company should define triggers before investment decisions are made.

| Trigger | Management response |
|---|---|
| Preferential tariff eligibility falls below the modeled threshold | Recalculate production routing and origin content |
| New origin rule requires materially higher regional value content | Localize additional fabrication or subassembly |
| One country faces a new tariff or trade restriction | Shift qualifying final assembly to the alternate hub |
| Port or border disruption exceeds the defined service threshold | Activate alternate port, route, or inventory buffer |
| Supplier single-source exposure exceeds the approved limit | Qualify a second supplier or redesign the component |
| New plant fails quality or delivery targets for two consecutive review periods | Freeze volume expansion and initiate remediation |
| Local incentives are withdrawn or delayed | Reapprove economics without incentives |
| Labor turnover exceeds the business-case assumption | Increase automation, training, or location alternatives |
| Customer rejects the new manufacturing source | Retain incumbent production and revise transfer scope |
| Engineering change backlog exceeds capacity | Slow transfers and add centralized manufacturing engineering |
| Political or regulatory risk reaches the company’s red-line level | Pause new capex and activate alternate footprint |
| Capacity utilization falls below the investment threshold | Defer expansion and use contract or shared capacity |

At least two qualified production routes should exist for products that are:

- Revenue-critical;
- Safety-critical;
- Subject to high tariff exposure;
- Difficult to requalify; or
- Dependent on a single country or port.

