# 10. Risk mitigations

## Tariff and trade risk

- Maintain a live tariff and origin database;
- Obtain binding customs rulings where available;
- Use supplier origin declarations;
- Audit regional-value calculations;
- Avoid origin claims based on minimal assembly;
- Include trade counsel in product-transfer decisions; and
- Preserve routing flexibility.

## Supplier risk

- Develop local suppliers in stages;
- Retain incumbent suppliers for critical parts during ramp-up;
- Establish dual tooling where economically justified;
- Use supplier scorecards;
- Audit quality systems and financial health;
- Create alternate specifications for non-proprietary components; and
- Maintain strategic safety stock for long-lead items.

## Quality and warranty risk

- Transfer mature products first;
- Use first-article and process-capability validation;
- Require layered audits;
- Track field failures by plant and supplier;
- Keep final test standards common;
- Require engineering signoff for process changes; and
- Maintain warranty reserves during the ramp period.

## Logistics risk

- Use more than one port and inland route where feasible;
- Maintain critical-component buffers;
- Establish regional postponement and configuration capacity;
- Use bonded warehouses where legally and economically appropriate;
- Model border delays and seasonal disruption; and
- Avoid concentrating all ASEAN output in one export corridor.

## Political and regulatory risk

- Conduct quarterly country-risk reviews;
- Include exit and suspension rights in leases and supplier contracts;
- Avoid excessive dependence on one incentive regime;
- Maintain local compliance and government-relations capability;
- Monitor labor, tax, customs, and local-content changes; and
- Establish a crisis-management playbook.

## People and capability risk

- Build supervisor and technician training academies;
- Use structured rotations from the incumbent hub;
- Offer retention packages for critical technical staff;
- Train local suppliers alongside plant teams;
- Document tacit manufacturing knowledge;
- Use multilingual work instructions; and
- Define succession plans for expatriate roles.

